# Auto-generated by SORADS Build Studio (slim build) - copy of sorads_gui/core/runtime_bootstrap.py
"""
runtime_bootstrap.py - SORADS "slim build" runtime: environment folder + library downloader for a built standalone EXE.

THIS FILE RUNS INSIDE THE BUILT EXE.  SORADS Build Studio copies it next to the .spec as the PyInstaller runtime hook
`pyi_rth_sorads_bootstrap.py` when "Slim build" is ticked in Step 3.  It uses the standard library only (tkinter for the window,
and pip - bundled into the EXE by the spec - for the downloads), so it also imports and runs unfrozen (tests, `python runtime_bootstrap.py`).

WHY
    Bundling the application AND all of its libraries (torch, numpy, PIL, ...) makes a huge EXE that is slow to build, slow to unpack
    (one-file) and slow to start.  A slim EXE contains only the application, Python and the bootstrap.  The libraries live in an
    ENVIRONMENT FOLDER outside the EXE, are downloaded once, and are only touched again when the requirement list changes.

TWO WAYS TO GET THE LIBRARIES (manifest "mode")
    local      the libraries are a COPY OF THE DEVELOPER'S ENVIRONMENT in a folder  <exe folder>/env  next to the EXE
               (a copied venv  env/Lib/site-packages,  env/lib/pythonX.Y/site-packages,  env/site-packages  or the packages directly in env).
               SORADS Build Studio can create that folder from the build venv after the build; or just copy your venv folder there.
               The EXE only checks it (milliseconds) and uses it - no window, no download. Missing libraries -> the window offers to download them.
    download   the libraries are downloaded once (pip) into an environment folder the user chooses on the first start.

WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE EXE STARTS
    1. A requirement list is read:   <exe folder>/requirements_runtime.txt  (only if someone put one there and it is not stale)
       else the requirements.txt INSIDE the application (<bundle>/sorads_runtime/requirements.txt), else the list in the manifest.
    2. The environment folder is resolved:   --env-dir PATH  >  %SORADS_ENV_DIR% / %<APP>_ENV_DIR%  >  <exe folder>/env  (when it exists and the
       build is "local", or nothing was saved yet)  >  the saved choice  >  %LOCALAPPDATA%/<App>/env
       (Linux: ~/.local/share/<App>/env,  macOS: ~/Library/Application Support/<App>/env).
       FIRST START (no saved choice): the setup window opens and ASKS for the folder - nothing is downloaded until you press
       "Install libraries" (the folder shown is only a suggestion). Your choice is saved, so later starts are silent.
    3. The folder is checked in milliseconds (dist-info folders are read, no import, no network):
         every library present in the right version   -> the folder is put on sys.path and the application starts, no window;
         something missing / its version changed       -> the setup window opens and downloads exactly that (pip --target, only wheels);
         "Show this window on every start" is ticked   -> the window opens anyway (it starts the app by itself after a short countdown).
    4. The folder's own pip cache (<env>/.pipcache) makes a reinstall fast, and nothing is downloaded when nothing is missing.
    The libraries are real files in the folder: DLL directories are registered, .pth files are processed, `__file__` works.

COMMAND LINE (stripped before the application sees its own arguments)
    --setup           open the setup window now          --no-setup   never open it (a missing library then fails like any ImportError)
    --env-dir PATH    use this environment folder        --update-libs   re-download every library of the list (upgrade)

FILES
    <data>/<App>/sorads_env.json          the user's choices: environment folder, show on startup, automatic updates, index URL
    <env>/site-packages-cpXY/             the libraries (one folder per Python version of the EXE; a new Python never reuses old binaries)
    <env>/sorads_env_stamp.json           what was installed, from which list (so a modified list is noticed)

CHILD PROCESSES: a multiprocessing child, a --pba-worker re-launch, or any process started by the application inherits
SORADS_BOOTSTRAP_DONE / SORADS_SITE_PACKAGES and only activates the libraries - it never shows a window.
"""
